**How lessons read data.** Each IRW table's landing page ([`verbagg`'s](https://itemresponsewarehouse.org/tables/verbagg/), for example) has a *Download CSV* link that needs no account; the lessons read it with `read.csv()`. The link names the data version (`v59_0`), so an analysis can be pinned to a release. The real-data code's `irw_csv()` finds the link. The [irw R package](https://itemresponsewarehouse.github.io/Rpkg/) does more but needs a free Redivis account (see *Going further*).

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
quiz({
  q: "In a long table, respondent 17 has no row for item 5. After reshaping to wide, what is in that cell, and what does it mean?",
  options: [
    {text: "NA; the table alone doesn't say whether item 5 was skipped or never shown", correct: true, why: "The long table records only the responses that exist. Why one is absent (a skip, a booklet design, running out of time) has to come from the documentation or the pattern of gaps."},
    {text: "0, because no response counts as wrong", correct: false, why: "Scoring an omission as wrong is a decision an analyst may make, but the reshape itself doesn't make it, and for an item that was never shown it would misstate what happened."},
    {text: "The reshape fails, because the matrix is incomplete", correct: false, why: "Wide layouts allow empty cells, and most real datasets have some."}
  ]
})
```

### A first look

With the data wide, one row per respondent, four descriptives give a first look at any table (item means are column means; sum scores are row sums):

1. **Missingness and categories, by item.** An item everyone answered alike says nothing about differences between respondents; an unused category can trouble polytomous models ([Polytomous items](polytomous.qmd)).
2. **Item means.** For 0/1 items, the proportion of 1s; on a test, a first measure of how easy an item is (the *p*-value of [Classical test theory and reliability](ctt-reliability.qmd), which [The Rasch model](rasch.qmd) replaces with a difficulty). A Likert mean is on the response scale, not a proportion.
3. **The sum-score distribution.** The simplest score: classical test theory is built on it, and the Rasch model needs nothing more. Its distribution shows any pile-up at floor or ceiling.
4. **Item-rest correlations**: each item against the sum of the *other* items, positive if the items share something. Including the item itself would inflate the correlation, more so on short tests (problem 1).

All four assume the items run the same way. Each lesson states its keying once: higher means more of the construct. Reverse-worded items must be flipped (on a 1–5 scale, $x \to 6 - x$) before sums or item-rest correlations mean anything.

#### Try it: keying

Six invented respondents rate four Mini-IPIP Extraversion items from 1 (strongly disagree) to 5 (strongly agree): "Am the life of the party", "Don't talk a lot", "Talk to a lot of different people at parties" and "Keep in the background" ([Donnellan et al., 2006](https://doi.org/10.1037/1040-3590.18.2.192); wording from the public-domain [IPIP key](https://ipip.ori.org/MiniIPIPKey.htm)). The second and fourth are worded in reverse.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof ky_key = Inputs.toggle({label: "Key the reversed items (6 − x)", value: false})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
{
  const items = ["Life of the party", "Don't talk a lot (R)", "Talk to many at parties", "Keep in the background (R)"];
  const raw = [[5, 1, 4, 2], [4, 2, 5, 3], [4, 3, 3, 2], [3, 2, 3, 4], [2, 4, 3, 4], [2, 5, 1, 4]];
  const X = raw.map(r => r.map((x, j) => (ky_key && j % 2 ? 6 - x : x)));
  const ir = itemRest(X);
  const cell = (x, on, bold) => `<td style="padding:3px 12px;text-align:center;${on ? `background:${palette.light};` : ""}${bold ? "font-weight:600;" : ""}">${x}</td>`;
  const head = `<tr><th style="padding:3px 12px">Respondent</th>${items.map(t => `<th style="padding:3px 12px;text-align:center">${t}</th>`).join("")}<th style="padding:3px 12px">Sum</th></tr>`;
  const rows = X.map((r, i) => `<tr>${cell(i + 1)}${r.map((x, j) => cell(x, ky_key && j % 2)).join("")}${cell(r.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0), false, true)}</tr>`).join("");
  const irRow = `<tr><td style="padding:3px 12px"><em>Item-rest r</em></td>${ir.map(v => `<td style="padding:3px 12px;text-align:center;font-weight:600;color:${v < 0 ? palette.contrast : palette.main}">${v.toFixed(2)}</td>`).join("")}<td></td></tr>`;
  const div = document.createElement("div");
  div.innerHTML = `<table>${head}${rows}${irRow}</table>`;
  return div;
}
```

Unkeyed, the sums barely differ (12 to 14), because agreeing with one kind of item cancels disagreeing with the other, and every item-rest correlation is negative. Keyed, the sums spread from 6 to 18, ordering respondents from most to least extraverted, and every item-rest correlation is positive.

